# Overrides the backend URL the frontend talks to. Leave unset for normal
# local dev -- it defaults to http://localhost:8000/api/, same as before
# this file existed.
#
# ngrok's free plan gives you exactly ONE public domain, so tunneling the
# frontend and backend as two separate URLs (the obvious-seeming approach)
# doesn't work on it -- the second `ngrok http` call fails with
# ERR_NGROK_334 ("endpoint already online"), since both would need that
# same one domain. The fix is to only tunnel the frontend (port 5173) and
# let Vite itself forward API/media requests to the local Django server
# (see vite.config.js's server.proxy) -- so set this to a *relative* path
# instead of a full URL:
#   1. ngrok http 5173 --url https://YOUR-DOMAIN.ngrok-free.dev
#   2. VITE_API_BASE_URL=/api/
# No second tunnel, no CORS, no ALLOWED_HOSTS entry for the tunnel's
# domain needed -- Django only ever sees requests from Vite's own proxy,
# which look identical to plain localhost traffic.
#
# On a paid ngrok plan with more than one domain available, you can still
# tunnel both separately instead: VITE_API_BASE_URL=https://....ngrok-free.app/api/
# (must end with a trailing slash, same as the default value), with
# `ngrok http 8000` for the backend and CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS/ALLOWED_HOSTS
# in backend/.env.example updated for both tunnel domains.
VITE_API_BASE_URL=
